{
  "dom_id": "step-q84036",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\nI. The number of wins of a football team in a $16$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $60%$\n\nII. The height of children in a class of $20$ students\n\nIII. The number of targets hit by an archer, out of $10$ targets total, if the archer has a $30%$ chance of hitting the target with any single shot.",
  "worked_solution": "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $16$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $16$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of targets hit (out of $10$ targets total) can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
